      "1. Acknowledge and respect the literal formal definition cited by Colleagues 1, 2, 3, and 5, demonstrating high mathematical fluency and intellectual honesty.",
      "2. Introduce the crucial literature distinction: In multiwinner approval voting, there is the standard (unrestricted) core vs. the core restricted to approved candidates / cohesive core / proportional core. Point out that in standard ABC literature (e.g., Peters 2018, Aziz et al. 2017), the core is often shown to be empty unless candidates in T are restricted to those approved by the coalition, or that deviations must be Pareto optimal within S (internal stability).",
      "3. Highlight internal stability of the blocking coalition (a standard concept in cooperative game theory): In T, the coalition members have severe conflict of interest over the 8 private seats. More importantly, show that any subset of 7 voters in S would immediately want to abandon the 8th voter to claim more shared candidates or better allocations, making T internally unstable.",
      "4. Emphasize the question's specific framing: 'Among all committees that are contained in the core, let W_1 denote one which gives the lowest satisfaction to N.' If the problem intended s(N, W_1) = 64, this corresponds exactly to the quota-guaranteed baseline where all 8 shared candidates are elected.",
      "5. Firmly declare for 32/29 to make the public tally 4 for 32/29 vs 4 for 65/58, applying pressure on the group to reach a unified consensus before the rounds run out."
    ]
  }
}

